[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Authentication and Authorization in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing your MongoDB database is crucial for protecting sensitive data. This tutorial covers authentication and authorization mechanisms in MongoDB, ensuring only authorized users can access specific data."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Authentication"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Authentication verifies the identity of users connecting to the MongoDB server. MongoDB supports several authentication mechanisms, including:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SCRAM-SHA-256: The default authentication mechanism since MongoDB 4.0. It provides strong security."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SCRAM-SHA-1: An older authentication mechanism, still supported for compatibility."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"x.509 Certificate Authentication: Uses client certificates for authentication."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LDAP Proxy Authentication: Integrates with LDAP servers for authentication."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To enable authentication, you need to configure the 'security.authorization' setting in the MongoDB configuration file (mongod.conf). Set it to 'enabled'."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"# mongod.conf
security:
  authorization: enabled"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"After enabling authorization, you must create an administrative user. Connect to the 'admin' database without authentication (initially) and create the user."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"use admin
db.createUser(
  {
    user: "admin",
    pwd:  "password123",
    roles: [ { role: "root", db: "admin" } ]
  }
)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Replace 'password123' with a strong password. After creating the administrative user, restart the MongoDB server. Now, authentication is required for all connections."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Authorization"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Authorization determines what actions authenticated users are allowed to perform. MongoDB uses role-based access control (RBAC). Users are assigned roles, and roles define specific privileges."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"MongoDB provides several built-in roles, such as:"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"dbOwner: Has full control over a specific database."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"read: Can read data from a database or collection."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"readWrite: Can read and write data to a database or collection."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"userAdmin: Can create and manage users within a database."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"clusterAdmin: Has administrative privileges at the cluster level."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also create custom roles to define specific sets of privileges. For example:"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"use testdb
db.createRole(
  {
    role: "backupRole",
    privileges: [
      { resource: { db: "testdb", collection: "data" }, actions: [ "find", "backup" ] }
    ],
    roles: []
  }
)"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"This creates a role named 'backupRole' that can 'find' and 'backup' data in the 'data' collection of the 'testdb' database."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"To assign a role to a user:"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"use testdb
db.createUser(
  {
    user: "backupUser",
    pwd: "securePassword",
    roles: [ { role: "backupRole", db: "testdb" } ]
  }
)"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Connecting with Authentication"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"When connecting to MongoDB after enabling authentication, you must provide the username and password. You can do this through the connection string:"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"mongodb://admin:password123@localhost:27017/admin"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Or, when using the MongoDB shell:"},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"mongo -u admin -p password123 --authenticationDatabase admin"},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use strong passwords and rotate them regularly."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Grant users only the necessary privileges (principle of least privilege)."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly audit user permissions and access logs."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using x.509 certificates or LDAP for enhanced security."},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"By implementing these authentication and authorization measures, you can significantly improve the security of your MongoDB database."}]
